# Phase 2 — Dynamic Content Layer: Frontmatter Validation with Zod

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a type-safe content management system on top of the local filesystem. Use `gray-matter` to split metadata from content and validate frontmatter headers using **Zod schemas** to catch missing titles, bad dates, or invalid tags at compile time.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Why Zod Schema Validation for Content?

In large teams, writers often make typos in Markdown headers (`author_image` vs `authorImage`, or malformed date strings). Unchecked, these cause silent runtime crashes during production builds.

Zod provides runtime contract enforcement:
```typescript
const PostFrontmatterSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string().min(5),
  date: z.string().regex(/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/),
  tags: z.array(z.string()),
  published: z.boolean().default(true),
});
```

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Create Content Model & Validation Schema

Create `lib/schemas.ts`:

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

export const PostSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string().min(1, 'Title is required'),
  publishedAt: z.string(),
  summary: z.string().min(10, 'Summary must be at least 10 chars'),
  tags: z.array(z.string()).default([]),
  author: z.object({
    name: z.string(),
    avatar: z.string().optional(),
  }).default({ name: 'Staff Engineer' }),
  draft: z.boolean().default(false),
});

export type PostFrontmatter = z.infer<typeof PostSchema>;

export interface PostItem extends PostFrontmatter {
  slug: string;
  readingTime: string;
  content: string;
}
```

### Step 2 · Build the File System Content Helper

Create `lib/mdx.ts`:

```typescript
import fs from 'fs';
import path from 'path';
import matter from 'gray-matter';
import { PostSchema, PostItem } from './schemas';

const POSTS_PATH = path.join(process.cwd(), 'content/posts');

export function getPostSlugs(): string[] {
  if (!fs.existsSync(POSTS_PATH)) return [];
  return fs.readdirSync(POSTS_PATH).filter((f) => /\.mdx?$/.test(f));
}

export function getPostBySlug(slug: string): PostItem {
  const cleanSlug = slug.replace(/\.mdx?$/, '');
  const filePath = path.join(POSTS_PATH, `${cleanSlug}.mdx`);
  const rawSource = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');

  const { data, content } = matter(rawSource);
  const validated = PostSchema.parse(data);

  // Calculate estimated reading time (~200 words per minute)
  const words = content.trim().split(/\s+/).length;
  const readingTime = `${Math.ceil(words / 200)} min read`;

  return {
    ...validated,
    slug: cleanSlug,
    readingTime,
    content,
  };
}

export function getAllPosts(): PostItem[] {
  return getPostSlugs()
    .map((f) => getPostBySlug(f))
    .filter((p) => !p.draft)
    .sort((a, b) => new Date(b.publishedAt).getTime() - new Date(a.publishedAt).getTime());
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] `getAllPosts()` parses and returns sorted articles newest first
- [ ] Adding an invalid frontmatter field triggers a clear Zod compile error
- [ ] Word count accurately calculates reading time

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** Validating CMS frontmatter with Zod turns documentation bugs into immediate compile-time errors instead of mysterious production build breaks.

# Phase 3 — Advanced MDX Compilation & Interactive React Component Injection

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Compile Markdown into React virtual DOM elements on the server using `next-mdx-remote/rsc`. Create and inject custom interactive React components (live code playground, tabs, interactive callouts) directly into Markdown prose.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### The Power of MDX

Standard Markdown is static. MDX allows embedding live interactive React components right into writing:

```mdx
# Understanding CSS Flexbox

Here is how `justify-content: space-between` behaves:

<InteractiveFlexDemo defaultDirection="row" />
```

`next-mdx-remote/rsc` compiles this securely on the server without using dangerous `eval()`.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Custom Interactive MDX Components

Create `components/mdx/Callout.tsx`:

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import { AlertCircle, CheckCircle2, Info } from 'lucide-react';

export function Callout({
  type = 'info',
  title,
  children,
}: {
  type?: 'info' | 'warning' | 'success';
  title?: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  const styles = {
    info: 'bg-sky-950/40 border-sky-800 text-sky-200',
    warning: 'bg-amber-950/40 border-amber-800 text-amber-200',
    success: 'bg-emerald-950/40 border-emerald-800 text-emerald-200',
  }[type];

  const icons = {
    info: <Info className="w-4 h-4 text-sky-400" />,
    warning: <AlertCircle className="w-4 h-4 text-amber-400" />,
    success: <CheckCircle2 className="w-4 h-4 text-emerald-400" />,
  }[type];

  return (
    <div className={`my-6 rounded-lg border p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed ${styles}`}>
      <div className="flex items-center gap-2 font-medium mb-1">
        {icons}
        <span>{title || type.toUpperCase()}</span>
      </div>
      <div className="text-stone-300">{children}</div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Step 2 · The MDX Renderer Island

Create `components/mdx/MDXRenderer.tsx`:

```tsx
import { MDXRemote } from 'next-mdx-remote/rsc';
import { Callout } from './Callout';
import Link from 'next/link';

const components = {
  Callout,
  a: ({ href, children, ...props }: any) => (
    <Link href={href || ''} className="text-amber-400 underline hover:text-amber-300" {...props}>
      {children}
    </Link>
  ),
};

export function MDXRenderer({ source }: { source: string }) {
  return (
    <article className="prose prose-invert prose-stone max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif prose-headings:text-stone-100 prose-pre:bg-stone-900 prose-pre:border prose-pre:border-stone-800">
      <MDXRemote source={source} components={components} />
    </article>
  );
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Adding `<Callout type="warning" title="Warning">Be careful</Callout>` in `.mdx` renders a styled warning card
- [ ] MDX parses without requiring client runtime JS for static typography

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** MDX turns passive reading into an interactive learning environment by seamlessly bridging text prose with living React code.

# Phase 4 — Syntax Highlighting, Line Numbers & Copy Code Islands

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement build-time syntax highlighting using **Rehype Pretty Code** and **Shiki** with themes like GitHub Dark. Add line numbering, highlighted line diffs, and an interactive copy-to-clipboard button that stays resilient across all code fences.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Build-Time vs Runtime Highlighting

Libraries like `highlight.js` running in the browser force the client to download grammar lexers for every language. **Shiki** evaluates syntax highlighting strictly on the server at build time, generating pure inline CSS spans (`<span style="color:#E1E4E8">`). The client downloads zero parsing JavaScript.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Copy Button Client Island

Create `components/mdx/CopyButton.tsx`:

```tsx
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';
import { Copy, Check } from 'lucide-react';

export function CopyButton({ text }: { text: string }) {
  const [copied, setCopied] = useState(false);

  const handleCopy = async () => {
    await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
    setCopied(true);
    setTimeout(() => setCopied(false), 2000);
  };

  return (
    <button
      onClick={handleCopy}
      aria-label="Copy code"
      className="absolute right-3 top-3 p-1.5 rounded-md border border-stone-700 bg-stone-800/80 text-stone-400 hover:text-stone-100 hover:border-stone-600 transition-colors"
    >
      {copied ? <Check className="w-3.5 h-3.5 text-emerald-400" /> : <Copy className="w-3.5 h-3.5" />}
    </button>
  );
}
```

### Step 2 · Pre Block Wrapper with Copy Button

Create `components/mdx/PreCodeBlock.tsx`:

```tsx
'use client';

import React, { useRef } from 'react';
import { CopyButton } from './CopyButton';

export function PreCodeBlock({ children, ...props }: any) {
  const preRef = useRef<HTMLPreElement>(null);

  return (
    <div className="relative group my-6">
      <pre ref={preRef} {...props} className="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-stone-800 bg-[#0d0d0d] p-4 text-xs font-mono">
        {children}
      </pre>
      <div className="opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity">
        <CopyButton text={preRef.current?.innerText || ''} />
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Code blocks render with dark syntax highlighting without runtime JS flashes
- [ ] Hovering over any code block reveals the copy button in top-right corner
- [ ] Clicking copy writes the exact raw text to clipboard and flashes a green checkmark

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** Shiki performs token highlighting during static rendering, ensuring syntax-colored code loads with zero layout shift.

# Phase 5 — Client-Side Full-Text Fuzzy Search Engine (Fuse.js)

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a lightning-fast client-side fuzzy search dialog triggered by `Cmd+K`. Generate a lightweight JSON search index at build time, and implement `Fuse.js` to match user typos across titles, summaries, and tags in < 2ms.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Why Static Client Search for Content Sites?

Setting up an external Elasticsearch or Algolia cluster for a blog of 50–500 articles adds infrastructure complexity and monthly bills. A client-side index of 100 articles is less than 40KB gzipped. Loading it into browser memory delivers sub-2ms instant search with zero network hops.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Build the Search Dialog Component

Create `components/SearchModal.tsx`:

```tsx
'use client';

import React, { useState, useEffect, useMemo } from 'react';
import Fuse from 'fuse.js';
import { Search, X, ArrowRight } from 'lucide-react';
import Link from 'next/link';

interface SearchItem {
  slug: string;
  title: string;
  summary: string;
  tags: string[];
}

export function SearchModal({ posts }: { posts: SearchItem[] }) {
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

  // Keyboard shortcut listener: Cmd+K / Ctrl+K
  useEffect(() => {
    const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
      if ((e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey) && e.key === 'k') {
        e.preventDefault();
        setIsOpen((prev) => !prev);
      }
      if (e.key === 'Escape') setIsOpen(false);
    };
    window.addEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);
  }, []);

  const fuse = useMemo(() => {
    return new Fuse(posts, {
      keys: ['title', 'summary', 'tags'],
      threshold: 0.35,
    });
  }, [posts]);

  const results = query.trim() ? fuse.search(query).map((r) => r.item) : [];

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  return (
    <div className="fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-start justify-center pt-24 bg-black/70 backdrop-blur-sm p-4">
      <div className="w-full max-w-xl bg-stone-900 border border-stone-800 rounded-xl shadow-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div className="flex items-center px-4 border-b border-stone-800">
          <Search className="w-4 h-4 text-stone-500 mr-3" />
          <input
            type="text"
            placeholder="Search articles, concepts, tags... (ESC to close)"
            value={query}
            onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)}
            autoFocus
            className="w-full py-3.5 bg-transparent text-sm text-stone-100 placeholder-stone-500 outline-none"
          />
          <button onClick={() => setIsOpen(false)} className="text-stone-500 hover:text-stone-300">
            <X className="w-4 h-4" />
          </button>
        </div>

        <div className="max-h-80 overflow-y-auto p-2 divide-y divide-stone-800/50">
          {query && results.length === 0 ? (
            <p className="p-4 text-xs text-stone-500 text-center">No matches found.</p>
          ) : (
            results.map((post) => (
              <Link
                key={post.slug}
                href={`/blog/${post.slug}`}
                onClick={() => setIsOpen(false)}
                className="group flex items-start justify-between p-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-stone-800 transition-colors"
              >
                <div>
                  <h4 className="text-sm font-medium text-stone-200 group-hover:text-amber-300">{post.title}</h4>
                  <p className="text-xs text-stone-400 line-clamp-1 mt-0.5">{post.summary}</p>
                </div>
                <ArrowRight className="w-4 h-4 text-stone-600 group-hover:text-amber-400 mt-1 shrink-0" />
              </Link>
            ))
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Pressing `Cmd+K` (or `Ctrl+K`) opens the modal with focused input
- [ ] Typing a query filters posts instantly using fuzzy matching (e.g. typing "closur" matches "closures")
- [ ] Pressing ESC closes the search dialog

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** Client-side index search provides instant, zero-latency feedback that delight developers accustomed to VS Code-speed navigation.

# Phase 6 — Sub-5ms View Count Analytics & Trending Articles via Redis

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Add real-time page view counts and a "Trending Articles" section using an atomic Redis counter. Implement an Edge API route that increments the view counter in < 5ms and deduplicates repeated refreshes within the same browser session.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Why Redis for View Counters?

If you increment an SQL row on every page view, traffic spikes will lock the row and destroy database throughput. Redis `INCR` is an atomic, in-memory operation that executes in less than 2 milliseconds and handles 100,000+ operations per second on modest hardware.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Connect Redis

Create `lib/redis.ts`:

```typescript
import { Redis } from '@upstash/redis';

export const redis = new Redis({
  url: process.env.UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL || 'http://localhost:8079',
  token: process.env.UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN || 'local_token',
});
```

### Step 2 · Build the View Counter API Route

Create `app/api/views/[slug]/route.ts`:

```typescript
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';
import { redis } from '@/lib/redis';

export async function POST(
  req: NextRequest,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }
) {
  const { slug } = await params;

  try {
    // Atomic increment
    const views = await redis.incr(`post:views:${slug}`);
    return NextResponse.json({ views });
  } catch (err) {
    return NextResponse.json({ views: 0 }, { status: 500 });
  }
}

export async function GET(
  req: NextRequest,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }
) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const views = (await redis.get<number>(`post:views:${slug}`)) || 0;
  return NextResponse.json({ views });
}
```

### Step 3 · View Counter Client Badge

Create `components/ViewCounter.tsx`:

```tsx
'use client';

import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Eye } from 'lucide-react';

export function ViewCounter({ slug }: { slug: string }) {
  const [views, setViews] = useState<number | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Fire POST to increment view on mount
    fetch(`/api/views/${slug}`, { method: 'POST' })
      .then((res) => res.json())
      .then((data) => setViews(data.views))
      .catch(() => setViews(0));
  }, [slug]);

  return (
    <span className="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs text-stone-500 font-mono">
      <Eye className="w-3.5 h-3.5 text-stone-600" />
      {views === null ? '...' : `${views.toLocaleString()} views`}
    </span>
  );
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Visiting a post page increments the Redis view counter atomically
- [ ] View count displays alongside reading time in post header
- [ ] Redis keys follow organized namespacing (`post:views:{slug}`)

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** Combining static CDN pages with a lightweight Edge Redis counter delivers real-time analytics without degrading page load speeds.

# Phase 7 — Dynamic OpenGraph Image Generation & RSS 2.0 Feed

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Generate dynamic, programmatic OpenGraph social share cards using `@vercel/og` (`ImageResponse`). Automatically generate an RSS 2.0 and Atom feed (`/feed.xml`) on build so developers can subscribe using Feedly or NetNewsWire.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Programmatic OpenGraph Cards

Static social images are boring. Dynamic OG cards render HTML/JSX into PNGs on the fly, rendering the exact article title, tags, reading time, and author avatar directly onto the Twitter/LinkedIn preview card.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Build the Dynamic OG Image Generator

Create `app/blog/[slug]/opengraph-image.tsx`:

```tsx
import { ImageResponse } from 'next/og';
import { getPostBySlug } from '@/lib/mdx';

export const runtime = 'edge';
export const size = { width: 1200, height: 630 };
export const contentType = 'image/png';

export default async function Image({ params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const post = getPostBySlug(slug);

  return new ImageResponse(
    (
      <div
        style={{
          height: '100%',
          width: '100%',
          display: 'flex',
          flexDirection: 'column',
          justifyContent: 'space-between',
          backgroundColor: '#0c0a09',
          padding: '80px',
          fontFamily: 'sans-serif',
        }}
      >
        <div style={{ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: '12px' }}>
          <div style={{ width: '16px', height: '16px', borderRadius: '50%', backgroundColor: '#f59e0b' }} />
          <span style={{ color: '#a8a29e', fontSize: '24px', letterSpacing: '2px' }}>DEV ENGINE</span>
        </div>

        <h1 style={{ fontSize: '64px', fontWeight: 'bold', color: '#f5f5f4', lineHeight: 1.1 }}>
          {post.title}
        </h1>

        <div style={{ display: 'flex', justifyContent: 'space-between', alignItems: 'center' }}>
          <span style={{ color: '#78716c', fontSize: '20px' }}>{post.publishedAt} · {post.readingTime}</span>
          <span style={{ color: '#d97706', fontSize: '20px' }}>{post.tags.join(' · ')}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    ),
    { ...size }
  );
}
```

### Step 2 · Build the Automated RSS Feed Route

Create `app/feed.xml/route.ts`:

```typescript
import { getAllPosts } from '@/lib/mdx';

export async function GET() {
  const posts = getAllPosts();
  const siteUrl = 'https://my-dev-blog.vercel.app';

  const rss = `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<rss version="2.0">
  <channel>
    <title>Developer Engineering Blog</title>
    <link>${siteUrl}</link>
    <description>Deep dives on Next.js, Node.js, and systems design.</description>
    <language>en</language>
    ${posts
      .map(
        (p) => `
    <item>
      <title><![CDATA[${p.title}]]></title>
      <link>${siteUrl}/blog/${p.slug}</link>
      <description><![CDATA[${p.summary}]]></description>
      <pubDate>${new Date(p.publishedAt).toUTCString()}</pubDate>
      <guid>${siteUrl}/blog/${p.slug}</guid>
    </item>`
      )
      .join('')}
  </channel>
</rss>`;

  return new Response(rss, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/xml; charset=utf-8' },
  });
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Visiting `/blog/[slug]/opengraph-image` renders a 1200x630 branded image with dynamic title
- [ ] Visiting `/feed.xml` returns a valid RSS XML document
- [ ] Social share crawlers (Twitter/Slack) automatically locate the preview card

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** Edge image generation turns social links into attractive, high-converting preview banners without maintaining image editing software.

# Phase 1 — Layered Clean Architecture & Scaffolding

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Scaffold an enterprise TypeScript API using clean layered architecture: strictly isolating Routers, Controllers, Services, and Repositories. Implement validated environment variables, global security middleware, and a comprehensive health-check endpoint.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### The Layered Clean Architecture

In amateur projects, developers write SQL queries directly inside Express route handlers. This tightly couples HTTP logic to database logic, making unit testing impossible and leading to spaghetti code.

```
HTTP Request ──► Router (Method + URL routing)
                    │
                    ▼
                 Controller (HTTP validation, status codes, response formatting)
                    │
                    ▼
                 Service (Pure business logic, orchestration, transactions)
                    │
                    ▼
                 Repository (Prisma / SQL database operations)
                    │
                    ▼
                 PostgreSQL Database
```

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Initialize TypeScript Project

```bash
mkdir enterprise-api && cd enterprise-api
npm init -y
```

Install dependencies:
```bash
npm install express cors helmet dotenv envalid pino pino-pretty redis @prisma/client
npm install -D typescript ts-node-dev @types/express @types/cors @types/node prisma
```

### Step 2 · TypeScript Config

Create `tsconfig.json`:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "CommonJS",
    "rootDir": "src",
    "outDir": "dist",
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  }
}
```

### Step 3 · Validated Configuration Module

Create `src/config/env.ts`:

```typescript
import { cleanEnv, str, port, url } from 'envalid';
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
dotenv.config();

export const env = cleanEnv(process.env, {
  NODE_ENV: str({ choices: ['development', 'test', 'production'], default: 'development' }),
  PORT: port({ default: 4000 }),
  DATABASE_URL: str(),
  REDIS_URL: str({ default: 'redis://localhost:6379' }),
  JWT_ACCESS_SECRET: str(),
  JWT_REFRESH_SECRET: str(),
  CORS_ORIGIN: str({ default: 'http://localhost:3000' }),
});
```

### Step 4 · Express App Initialization

Create `src/index.ts`:

```typescript
import express from 'express';
import cors from 'cors';
import helmet from 'helmet';
import { env } from './config/env';

const app = express();

app.use(helmet());
app.use(cors({ origin: env.CORS_ORIGIN, credentials: true }));
app.use(express.json());

app.get('/health', (_req, res) => {
  res.json({
    status: 'healthy',
    uptime: process.uptime(),
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
  });
});

app.listen(env.PORT, () => {
  console.log(`🚀 Enterprise API active on http://localhost:${env.PORT}`);
});

export default app;
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] `npm run dev` starts without TypeScript compilation errors
- [ ] Omitting `DATABASE_URL` in `.env` causes immediate startup failure with clear diagnostic error
- [ ] `GET /health` returns JSON status with uptime

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** Clean layered architecture separates HTTP transport concerns from business rules, ensuring every layer can be tested in complete isolation.

# Phase 3 — Dual-Token Authentication: JWT + Refresh Token Rotation with Redis

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement industry-standard dual-token authentication. Issue short-lived Access Tokens (15 mins) and long-lived Refresh Tokens (7 days). Implement Refresh Token Rotation and store blacklisted tokens in Redis to enable instant session revocation.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

### Why Dual Tokens?

- **Access Tokens** are verified statelessly using cryptography without touching a database. But if an access token is compromised, an attacker has access until it expires.
- Setting a short expiry (15m) minimizes the risk window.
- **Refresh Tokens** allow seamless token renewal. If a stolen refresh token is used, **Token Family Rotation** detects reuse, invalidates the entire family, and forces re-authentication.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Install Cryptography Packages

```bash
npm install jsonwebtoken bcryptjs
npm install -D @types/jsonwebtoken @types/bcryptjs
```

### Step 2 · Token Service

Create `src/services/token.service.ts`:

```typescript
import jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';
import { env } from '../config/env';

export interface TokenPayload {
  userId: string;
  role: string;
}

export class TokenService {
  public static generateAccessToken(payload: TokenPayload): string {
    return jwt.sign(payload, env.JWT_ACCESS_SECRET, { expiresIn: '15m' });
  }

  public static generateRefreshToken(payload: TokenPayload): string {
    return jwt.sign(payload, env.JWT_REFRESH_SECRET, { expiresIn: '7d' });
  }

  public static verifyAccessToken(token: string): TokenPayload {
    return jwt.verify(token, env.JWT_ACCESS_SECRET) as TokenPayload;
  }

  public static verifyRefreshToken(token: string): TokenPayload {
    return jwt.verify(token, env.JWT_REFRESH_SECRET) as TokenPayload;
  }
}
```

### Step 3 · Authentication Middleware

Create `src/middleware/authenticate.ts`:

```typescript
import { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';
import { TokenService } from '../services/token.service';

declare global {
  namespace Express {
    interface Request {
      user?: { userId: string; role: string };
    }
  }
}

export function authenticate(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction): void {
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;
  if (!authHeader?.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication required' });
    return;
  }

  const token = authHeader.split(' ')[1];
  try {
    req.user = TokenService.verifyAccessToken(token);
    next();
  } catch {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired access token' });
  }
}
```

---

